# Line endings, pinned rather than inherited from whoever cloned the repo.
#
# WHY THIS FILE CHANGED NOTHING ON THE DAY IT LANDED. `git ls-files --eol` on 2026-09-15 reported
# the index already uniformly LF -- 1,444 files i/lf w/lf, 1,372 i/lf w/crlf, 13 i/lf w/mixed, 4
# genuine binaries. Not one file needed `git add --renormalize`. The divergence was entirely on
# the worktree side, from `core.autocrlf=true` on the author's machine, which is why every commit
# emitted "LF will be replaced by CRLF" and why nothing downstream had noticed.
#
# WHY IT IS WORTH HAVING ANYWAY. The CI added in 02a305f runs on ubuntu-latest and has never run.
# Several tests hash or byte-compare tracked text -- tests/unit/test_cftc_cot_fixture.py on a gzip
# header, tests/integration/test_binance_fixture.py:269, tests/unit/test_assembled_strategy.py:236
# -- and "passes here, fails there" from a line ending is the least interesting way to spend a
# first CI run. Declaring the convention costs nothing and removes the class.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to translate them. The panels (*.parquet, *.npz, *.csv.gz, *.zip)
# left the index in D536 and these rules should outlive that: they will be right again the moment
# anything of those types is committed, and they cost nothing while nothing is.
*.png     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.parquet binary
*.npz     binary
*.gz      binary
*.zip     binary

# SIX TRACKED FILES ARE NOT UTF-8, AND `* text=auto eol=lf` ABOVE ASSERTS THAT THEY ARE (D543).
#
# `text=auto` is content detection, not a declaration, and git's heuristic is "does it contain
# NUL". None of the first five does, so all five are classified text and line-ending-normalised
# by a tool that cannot read their bytes. The sixth escapes only by accident -- it has 386 NULs,
# because it is a PDF.
#
# Naming them `binary` does not change the bytes. It stops git claiming to understand them, and
# it stops `git add --renormalize` or a fresh checkout from round-tripping a cp1252 file through
# a text filter. THE COST IS REAL AND IS THE POINT OF WRITING IT DOWN: git will no longer diff
# these files, so a future change to one becomes invisible in review. For frozen evidence that
# is the right trade -- three of the six are captured console output and none of the six is
# parsed by any `.py` in the tree.
#
# Encodings identified by byte inspection, not guessed: the first four carry only 0x92/0x93/0x94
# (smart quotes) or 0x97 (em dash), which are cp1252 -- in latin-1 those are C1 control codes.
data/D3_french_variable_definitions.html          binary
data/d279_run.log                                 binary
data/d279_run_corrected.log                       binary
data/d285_run.log                                 binary

# cp1252/latin-1 family (0xe1 0xe9 0xed -- a, e, i with acute; identical in both, so this one is
# genuinely indeterminate). LOAD-BEARING: cited by D386 and by three documents under
# docs/research/Prop-Firm-080926/. It is lossy `pdftotext` output that has already dropped the
# Czech diacritics it could not carry, which is exactly why its remaining bytes should not be
# put through another lossy step.
data/ftmo_challenge_terms_2026-08-04.txt          binary

# NOT HTML. The first eight bytes are `%PDF-1.5`; the extension is wrong, not the encoding. Git
# already infers binary from its NULs, so this line changes nothing today and states the fact
# rather than leaving it to a heuristic. Renaming it is the principal's call, not this file's.
data/k4_sec_34-81446_2017_ex_date_transition.html binary
